A||||2||Mark||It started with an exhaust leak...||||1||
Z||000000||Mark||07-15-2000||11:55 PM||||I noticed the sound of an exhaust leak in my '86 GT.  Not the "ticking" sound usually associated with a cracked manifold, but an "escaping air" sound you might typically expect from a leaking joint.  I removed the exhaust system from the wye pipe back (actually a pretty easy job) for a close visual inspection and found no obvious signs of damage or a leak.  With the cat converter now off, and with much better access to the front manifold, I did find evidence of leakage at all three ports.  I pulled the manifold for closer inspection and found it was in good shape (no cracks).  The gasket was intact and looked decent, except for the carbon tracks at the leak points.  Just a case of a bad gasket or improper installation (maybe during the 88-C-24 recall work)? <P>Additionally, with the wye pipe connected at the EGR valve and rear manifold, I am able to wiggle it slightly up and down from the front manifold port). It appears that the EGR tube flexes at the plenum flange.  Is this normal?  The EGR tube looks brand new (was apparently replaced by the previous owner).<P>I plan to have the manifold checked for flatness at the three flanges and fixed if necessary.  Any recommendations on exhaust manifold gaskets?  Should I pull the plenum and inspect the EGR tube?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Brian||07-16-2000||06:57 AM||||Mark,<P>The exhaust manifold gaskets you want to use are GM 10051512 (high heat).<BR>The movement of the EGR tube at the plenum end flange sounds a bit strange. I'd check that there is indeed a gasket there & that the bolts are snug. <P>If need be the tube can be pulled without removing the plenum. If I recall correctly pulling the distributor cap to gain a bit of room will allow a 1/4" universal & an extension to get in there. It's been a while but I seem to remember snaking that combo up behind the heat shielding?<P>Good luck,<BR>Brian||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||07-16-2000||10:22 AM||||I would definitely look into the EGR tube gasket situation. I had a horrible time when I rebuilt my motor the first time, with that tube leaking. I think I must have pulled that sucker 4 or 5 times. Finally ended up buying some heavy duty exhaust gasket material and making my own gaskets, which finally quieted it down...(well, as quiet as that motor was EVER gonna be anyway)<BR>I'd say looseness in the tube = a leak at the tube.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
